# ============================================================================
# EXPERIMENT 5 - two further table kinds from the substrate  [REPAIRED]
# (a) the TIME-INDEXED TABLE: one static tau value whose row set differs by
#     epoch (constant time constraints inside the rows) - SELECT answers
#     change over time on an unchanging value
# (b) the FUNCTIONAL-DEPENDENCY table: what a fixed schema can and CANNOT say
# ============================================================================
# REPAIR NOTE (Opus, 2026-09-01). The original (a) used CONJUNCTIVE time
# guards inside the row constants, e.g. { [t < 3] && oid[t] = 1 }. A tau
# constant is read under an implicit `always`, so that reads "for all t,
# t < 3" - false at t = 3 - and the whole row collapses to 0. The original
# file therefore claimed T T T F for four DEAD values; the binary answered
# F F F F and only the fourth "control" accidentally agreed. Verified:
#   n { [t < 3] && oid[t]:bv[8] = { #x01 }:bv[8] } = 0    ->  T
# The repair is the IMPLICATION form, which is the same shape the run-level
# guard in exp2 (c) already used: the guard scopes the row to its epoch
# instead of asserting the epoch.
#   n { [t < 3] -> oid[t]:bv[8] = { #x01 }:bv[8] } != 0   ->  T
#
# REPAIR NOTE 2. The original (b) proved two propositional tautologies: the
# first restated a hypothesis as its conclusion ("return h"), the second
# asked for a violation of an FD it had already assumed. Neither said
# anything about tables. They are replaced by the honest content: a fixed
# ADT schema CANNOT enforce a functional dependency (M6), so an FD must be
# carried as an explicit hypothesis - and once carried, real consequences
# are derivable from it (M7, transitivity, with its non-vacuity witness M8).
#
# H5a: rows guarded by [t<N] -> ... give an epoch-versioned table: the same
# reading is entailed inside its epoch and IMPOSSIBLE outside it.
# H5b: uniqueness is not a property of the schema; it is a hypothesis.
# Columns are sbf here on purpose: mixing constant time constraints with
# bitvector columns makes the engine print a cvc5 translation failure
# on STDOUT while still returning a verdict and exit code 0 - see
# opus_tau/opus_cvc5_faildopen.md. Query (a6) keeps one bitvector claim in
# the meet-only form, which does not trip the translator.
# Run: tau -q < exp5_time_indexed_tables.tau     (0.7.0-alpha 0ac2756+)
# EXPECTED-RESULTS: T T T T F T T T T

set charvar off
set maxsplits 1

# --- (a) time-indexed table -------------------------------------------------
# TT is ONE value. Before step 3 the key ohi carries payload oq = 1; from
# step 3 on the same key carries oq = 0. No clock logic, no second table.

# (a1) the table is live:
n { ([t < 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=1)) && ([t >= 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=0)) } != 0
# Answer: T

# (a2) TT ENTAILS the early reading (membership, not mere overlap):
n ({ ([t < 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=1)) && ([t >= 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=0)) } & { [t < 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=1) }') = 0
# Answer: T - inside its epoch the early reading is forced.

# (a3) TT ENTAILS the late reading:
n ({ ([t < 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=1)) && ([t >= 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=0)) } & { [t >= 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=0) }') = 0
# Answer: T - and after the boundary the late reading is forced.

# (a4) CONTROL - the CROSS-EPOCH FORGERY is not merely unproven, it is
# IMPOSSIBLE: claiming the early payload in the late window meets TT at 0.
n ({ ([t < 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=1)) && ([t >= 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=0)) } & { [t >= 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=1) }) = 0
# Answer: T

# (a5) CONTROL - and so it is certainly not entailed (the negative twin of
# a2/a3: a T here would mean the forgery was a member of the table):
n ({ ([t < 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=1)) && ([t >= 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=0)) } & { [t >= 3] -> (ohi[t]=1 && oq[t]=1) }') = 0
# Answer: F

# (a6) the same impossibility with real bitvector prices (10 early, 99 late),
# stated as a meet so the cvc5 translator is not involved:
n ({ ([t < 3] -> (oid[t]:bv[8] = { #x01 }:bv[8] && oqty[t]:bv[8] = { #x0A }:bv[8])) && ([t >= 3] -> (oid[t]:bv[8] = { #x01 }:bv[8] && oqty[t]:bv[8] = { #x63 }:bv[8])) } & { [t >= 3] -> (oid[t]:bv[8] = { #x01 }:bv[8] && oqty[t]:bv[8] = { #x0A }:bv[8]) }) = 0
# Answer: T - a stale price cannot be selected after the epoch boundary.

# --- (b) functional dependency: what the schema does NOT give you -----------

# (b1) THE NEGATIVE RESULT: the schema alone does not enforce "id determines
# qty". A table with equal ids and unequal quantities exists:
type Row = {id: bv[8], qty: bv[8]}. type Tab = {r1: Row, r2: Row}. n ex t:Tab (t.r1.id = t.r2.id && t.r1.qty != t.r2.qty)
# Answer: T - uniqueness is NOT a property of an ADT table type. It has to
# be carried as a hypothesis in every query that needs it.

# (b2) ...and once carried, it has real consequences. Over three rows the FD
# is transitive - the conclusion appears in no hypothesis:
type Row = {id: bv[8], qty: bv[8]}. type Tab3 = {r1: Row, r2: Row, r3: Row}. n all t:Tab3 ((((t.r1.id = t.r2.id) -> (t.r1.qty = t.r2.qty)) && ((t.r2.id = t.r3.id) -> (t.r2.qty = t.r3.qty)) && t.r1.id = t.r2.id && t.r2.id = t.r3.id) -> t.r1.qty = t.r3.qty)
# Answer: T

# (b3) NON-VACUITY for b2 - the hypotheses are jointly satisfiable:
type Row = {id: bv[8], qty: bv[8]}. type Tab3 = {r1: Row, r2: Row, r3: Row}. n ex t:Tab3 (((t.r1.id = t.r2.id) -> (t.r1.qty = t.r2.qty)) && ((t.r2.id = t.r3.id) -> (t.r2.qty = t.r3.qty)) && t.r1.id = t.r2.id && t.r2.id = t.r3.id)
# Answer: T
